\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1}Introduction}{1}{section.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Same-text intervention isolating continued access after full exposure. The prompt itself is unchanged. In \textup  {Full}{}, every generated query retains ordinary causal access to the known-wrong draft. In \textup  {LateBlock}{}, the complete prompt is processed with ordinary causal attention, and $y_1$ is sampled from the same full-prefill logits as in \textup  {Full}{}. Only then does blocking begin: the query at the $y_1$ position, which predicts $y_2$, is the first affected query. It and all subsequent generated queries cannot directly attend to the failed-draft key--value positions. Dashed arrows and crosses denote the removed attention edges. Access to the problem, error signal, and independent-solve instruction is preserved.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:continued-access-overview}{{1}{2}{Same-text intervention isolating continued access after full exposure. The prompt itself is unchanged. In \fullaccess {}, every generated query retains ordinary causal access to the known-wrong draft. In \lateblock {}, the complete prompt is processed with ordinary causal attention, and $y_1$ is sampled from the same full-prefill logits as in \fullaccess {}. Only then does blocking begin: the query at the $y_1$ position, which predicts $y_2$, is the first affected query. It and all subsequent generated queries cannot directly attend to the failed-draft key--value positions. Dashed arrows and crosses denote the removed attention edges. Access to the problem, error signal, and independent-solve instruction is preserved}{figure.1}{}}
